Honour any worker count: 1, 3, 32 — never silently rewritten to 8

The setting was capped or rewritten in five separate places: the service clamped the worker count
at 8, both settings pages carried max="8" and Math.min(8, ...) on save, and the batch view
reported a hard 16. Anyone asking for 32 got 8 without being told.

The ceiling is now one documented constant (SubSyncService.MaxParallelWorkers = 64), stated in
the settings text rather than kept secret, and it exists only so a mistyped value (10000) cannot
spawn thousands of processes. Every value in range is used exactly as written: 1 means one, 3
means three, 32 means thirty-two. The batch view advertises the ceiling so the pages clamp
against the same number the server uses, instead of each carrying its own copy; the worker list
scrolls, so a 32-row batch stays readable.

Harness: 111 checks, ALL PASS, including "1 worker stays 1", "3 workers stay 3", "32 workers
stay 32", "a mistyped value falls back to the ceiling, not to 8", and the pool filling to 3, 16,
32 and 64 jobs when the queue has them.

Version 1.1.0.28.
